WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.450
- [Instructor] This right over here

00:00:01.450 --> 00:00:04.840
is an image from an exercise
on Khan Academy and it says

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:08.400
compare using greater than,
less than, or equal to.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.070
And on the left, we have 1/4 times 5,000,

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.370
and we wanna compare that to 5,000.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:15.590
On Khan Academy, you would
click on this drop-down

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:18.460
and you would pick greater
than, less than, or equal to.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:20.310
So what do you think it is?

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:23.600
How does 1/4 times 5,000 compare to 5,000?

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.280
And how do you know
your answer is correct?

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.520
Pick one of these choices as well.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.420
Pause the video and have a go at that.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:30.470
Well, one way to think about it,

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:33.490
if I'm multiplying 5,000 times some value,

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:37.530
that value is going to scale 5,000.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:40.100
It's going to make it smaller or bigger

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.790
or in certain cases, might
not change the size at all.

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:44.401
In fact, the ones where
you don't change 5,000

00:00:44.401 --> 00:00:46.660
was you multiply it by one.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:50.980
So one times 5,000 is equal to 5,000.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:52.400
But what do you think is going to happen

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.270
when you multiply a value

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.990
that is between zero and one times 5,000?

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:59.150
Is that going to be
greater than, equal to,

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:01.370
or less than 5,000?

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:03.650
Well one way to imagine
it is on a number line.

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:06.823
If this is zero, this is 5,000.

00:01:06.823 --> 00:01:11.823
This would be halfway
between zero and 5,000.

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:16.720
And if I then make it the
space between zero and 5,000

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.330
into fourths, this would be 1/4 of the way

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:21.150
between zero and 5,000.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:22.680
So this value right over here,

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:27.020
that is 1/4 times 5,000.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:30.140
Well, how does this value,

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:35.140
1/4 times 5,000, compare
to this value, to 5,000?

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:39.510
Well, it's clear that 1/4 times 5,000

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:41.590
is less than,

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:44.100
is less than 5,000.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:47.980
And in general, if the value
is between zero and one

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:49.680
that you're multiplying by something else,

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.500
it's going to make the thing
you're multiplying smaller.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.780
If this were one, it's not
going to change the value.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:57.740
And if this were greater than one,

00:01:57.740 --> 00:01:59.590
it would make it larger.

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:00.620
So let's see these choices.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.440
Because we are multiplying
5,000 by a fraction

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:05.320
that is less than one.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:06.500
Yeah, that's exactly what's happening.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:09.310
We're multiplying 5,000 by 1/4.

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:12.110
So it's going to give us a
smaller value than 5,000.

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:13.790
I like that choice.

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:14.900
Let's just read these other ones.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:16.950
Because we are multiplying
5,000 by a fraction

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.063
that is equal to one.

00:02:18.063 --> 00:02:21.260
Well no, 1/4 is clearly not equal to one,

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:22.670
so we rule that one out.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:24.590
Because we are multiplying
5,000 by a fraction

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:25.790
that is greater than one.

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:28.060
Well 1/4 is clearly not greater than one,

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:29.690
so we rule that out as well.

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:30.993
Let's do another example.

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:33.660
So here we're asked,

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:36.416
is the product of each
expression less than,

00:02:36.416 --> 00:02:39.420
equal to, or greater than 49?

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:42.650
So pause this video and
try to figure it out.

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:46.163
All right, so this first
one is 49 times 7/8.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:49.500
So what do you think that's going to be?

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:52.980
Well, 7/8 is less than one.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:56.610
So if I multiply 49 times
something less than one,

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:59.630
I'm going to get a value
that is less than 49.

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:02.970
So I like that choice right over there.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.506
49 times 5/2.

00:03:05.506 --> 00:03:08.780
Well, 5/2 is greater than one.

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:10.710
Remember, 2/2 would be equal to one.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:12.920
So 5/2 is greater than one.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:15.010
So if I'm multiplying 49 by a value

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:17.420
that is greater than one,
I'm going to get a value

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:19.123
that is greater than 49.

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:25.996
And then last but not least, 49 times 3/3.

00:03:25.996 --> 00:03:29.540
Well, 3/3 is exactly equal to one.

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:34.463
So 49 times one is going
to be exactly equal to 49,

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:36.893
and we're done.

